  "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385/assets/eeadcddee3bc966372613ded9304c723768e9b81cd0299ad14084a94c9348463.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
